<h2>The allegations and the defence</h2>
<p>The FIR, registered in Bhagalpur district in 2023, alleged that the petitioner kidnapped the informant\'s minor daughter — then about seventeen — for illicit intercourse or marriage, invoking Sections 366A, 120B and 34 of the IPC and Section 8 of the POCSO Act. The petitioner, in custody since 30 January 2026, said the two were in a relationship the girl\'s family opposed on account of religious difference; that she left home of her own volition in March 2023; that the two married; and that a radiological age estimate placed her between seventeen and nineteen. The chargesheet had been filed, removing, the defence argued, any realistic apprehension of evidence tampering.</p>

<h2>The amicus\'s role</h2>
<p>With the informant absent despite notice, Justice Chandra Shekhar Jha appointed an amicus curiae to examine the record. Her analysis was decisive: on the victim\'s own statements under Sections 161 and 164 CrPC, the allegations of kidnapping and sexual assault were not borne out, and the victim had stated that the relationship predated the FIR by about two years. The order records, in terms, that the allegation of kidnapping and sexual assault was &ldquo;not available&rdquo; against the petitioner on that material.</p>

<div class="flow">
<div class="fstep"><strong>FIR version</strong> — kidnapping of a minor for illicit purposes, by the informant\'s account.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Victim\'s recorded statements</strong> — a pre-existing relationship, voluntary departure, no support for assault allegations.</div>
<div class="fstep"><strong>Bail decision</strong> — custody since January 2026 plus an unsupportive prosecutrix equals release on bond, with the trial left to run its course.</div>
</div>

<h2>What the order illustrates</h2>
<p>First, the early statements matter more than almost anything else in the bail calculus for sexual-offence cases. Where the Section 164 statement contradicts the FIR\'s core, courts increasingly decline to keep an accused in custody on the strength of the FIR alone. Second, the procedural device of the amicus curiae protects both sides: the absent informant\'s interest is independently represented, and the accused is not prejudiced by a one-sided hearing. Third, bail orders of this kind are deliberately narrow — the Court granted release on a bond of Rs. 10,000 with two sureties before the Special POCSO Court at Bhagalpur, subject to the conditions in Section 480(3) of the BNSS, and expressed no final view on guilt.</p>

<div class="check">
<p><strong>Practice points for the defence:</strong> obtain and study the Section 164 statement at the earliest stage; frame the bail plea around what the victim has actually said rather than around the FIR\'s characterisation; place age-determination material on record where age is contested; and where the informant is not appearing, assist the court in ensuring the record itself — not the absence — does the talking.</p>
</div>

<div class="note">
<p>POCSO prosecutions where the alleged victim does not support the case present courts with a recurring dilemma. The answer at the bail stage is not to pre-judge the trial but to ask whether continued custody is justified on the material as it stands. On this record, the Patna High Court held it was not.</p>
</div>
